public inherited sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> errors = new Map<Id, String>();
        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return errors;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
        ];

        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isBlank(c.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }
            c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
        }

        // partialSuccess: true – a failed validation rule doesn't block the others.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(contacts, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                String msg = sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage();
                errors.put(contacts[i].Id, msg);
            }
        }

        return errors;
    }
}
```

Note on your approach: it's solid, and I only adjusted one thing — `Database.update(contacts, false)` returns results in the same order as the input list, so I index `contacts[i]` against `results[i]` to correlate each failed record with its first error. Also, `inherited sharing` on the class keeps DML/queries running in the caller's sharing context (use `with sharing` if you prefer stricter default behavior). Finally, the SOQL filters nothing on sharing but re-queries only the contacts that exist; IDs for missing/nonexistent contacts simply won't appear in results or the error map, which is the safest bulk-safe interpretation for a 200-contact batch.

