public inherited sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends a renewal reminder email to the owner of each given opportunity.
     * Returns the number of opportunities for which an email was successfully sent.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppsById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [
                SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
                FROM Opportunity
                WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
            ]
        );

        // Keep only opportunities whose owner is an active User.
        Set<Id> ownerIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : oppsById.values()) {
            if (opp.OwnerId != null) {
                ownerIds.add(opp.OwnerId);
            }
        }
        Set<Id> validOwnerIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (User u : [
            SELECT Id FROM User WHERE Id IN :ownerIds AND IsActive = true
        ]) {
            validOwnerIds.add(u.Id);
        }

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> emails =
            new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        List<Id> oppsEmailed = new List<Id>();

        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            Opportunity opp = oppsById.get(oppId);
            if (opp == null || !validOwnerIds.contains(opp.OwnerId)) {
                continue;
            }

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            email.setSaveAsActivity(false); // emails to internal users by Id don't count against the external daily cap
            email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            email.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hello,\n\n'
                + 'The renewal opportunity "' + opp.Name
                + '" has its renewal due on ' + opp.CloseDate.format()
                + '.\n\nPlease take the necessary action.\n\nThank you.'
            );
            emails.add(email);
            oppsEmailed.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (emails.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // One call for the whole batch: bulk-safe and well within the
        // 10 sendEmail-calls-per-transaction limit. Invalid recipients are
        // reported per message via SendEmailError, so one bad address does
        // not prevent the others from being sent.
        Integer sent = 0;
        try {
            List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> results = Messaging.sendEmail(emails, false);
            for (Messaging.SendEmailResult result : results) {
                if (result.isSuccess()) {
                    sent++;
                }
            }
        } catch (System.EmailException e) {
            // Entire call rejected (e.g., no valid recipients at all).
            sent = 0;
        }
        return sent;
    }
}
```

**Summary of the feedback on your approach:**

1. **The loop-and-send design breaks at 11 records** due to the 10 `sendEmail` calls/transaction limit, and `LimitException` is uncatchable — the whole scheduled run would die. The batched call above avoids this.
2. **Per-record calls aren't needed for failure isolation** — the default (`allOrNothing=false`) already sends the valid messages and reports failures per recipient via `SendEmailResult`/`SendEmailError`.
3. **Guard against inactive/missing owners** — sends to such Ids throw, and `CloseDate`/name could also be null.
4. Minor nicety: `setSaveAsActivity(false)` is correct (required when targeting internal users, and keeps you off the external email cap as you noted), and `inherited sharing` keeps the service safe for both scheduled and user-context invocation.

I'm going to change your approach, and here's why: loading **all** contacts into a static map at the top of the handler is exactly the anti-pattern bulkification is meant to avoid. Your org has ~2.3 million contacts; a single unfiltered `SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact` will blow past the 50,000-row query limit immediately and throw a `System.LimitException` (uncatchable) on the very first MDM batch. "No queries in loops" doesn't mean "one giant query" — it means "one query per transaction, scoped to the records you actually need."

The bulk-safe pattern is:

1. In the trigger, filter `Trigger.new`/`Trigger.oldMap` to accounts where `BillingCountry` actually changed.
2. Collect their Ids and run **one** SOQL query for `Contact WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds` — no loop, one query, and it only touches the handful of contacts relevant to a 200-account batch (MDM batches mean you'll typically query a few hundred to a few thousand rows, far under all governor limits).
3. Compute updates in memory, then issue **one** `update` DML on the collected list.

This also naturally avoids the SOQL-in-loop problem, since the contact query happens once per transaction. Bonus: scoping the query means we don't hold references to millions of sObjects in the heap, and we don't waste a full table scan on every account update.

One more refinement: `BillingCountry` is a standard text field and can be null. I treat null → null (no change), null → value and value → null as changes, and compare case-insensitively via `equalsIgnoreCase` since country values are free text (e.g., "USA" vs "usa" would otherwise silently count as "changed").

